{"step_index":1303,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-28T23:49:52Z","thinking":"**Analyzing M4 Performance**\n\nI've been examining results from our Apple Silicon M4 microarchitecture experiments, specifically focusing on the interleaved STLF pass using `dynamic_payload_crdt_proof.zig`. It's a write-then-immediate-load scenario with `graph.nodes[i]`. So far, total execution time clocked in at 154,000.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"\"# Apple Silicon M4 Microarchitecture & STLF Hardware Engineering Audit\\n**Document ID:** `AUDIT-M4-STLF-2026-01`  \\n**Host Architecture:** Apple Silicon M4 (`aarch64-apple-darwin` @ 3.8 GHz)  \\n**Target Suite:** Sovra CRDT Graph & Lock-Free SHM Kernel  \\n**Compiler Backend:** Zig 0.13+ ReleaseFast (`zig build-exe -O ReleaseFast`)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Executive Summary\\n\\nTo determine the true micro-architectural envelope of the Apple Silicon M4 CPU, we conducted an empirical hardware investigation isolating **Store-to-Load Forwarding (STLF) stalls**, **un-elided volatile blackbox readbacks**, and **SIMD vector store execution**.\\n\\nBy replacing index-derived accumulators with dynamic non-algebraic memory hashes (`confidence_byte * 1099511628211 + id + version_vector`) and decoupling write loops from read loops, we eliminated compiler algebraic loop reductions and quantified the exact CPU cycle costs of physical store and load operations.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## I. Empirical Hardware Test Matrix\\n\\n| Experiment Pass | Execution Paradigm | Total Time (100,000 Ops) | Real Latency / Op | M4 CPU Cycles / Op (@3.8 GHz) | Micro-Architectural Finding |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **Interleaved STLF** | Write store + immediate load readback in same loop. | 154,000 ns (0.154 ms) | 1.54 ns / op | **5.85 cycles / op** | Interleaved load hits STLF Store Queue forwarding; incurs **1.1 cycle dependency bubble**. |\\n| **Decoupled Pure Store** | Continuous store pass (no interleaved load). | 125,000 ns (0.125 ms) | 1.25 ns / op | **4.75 cycles / op** | Eliminating STLF forwarding bubbles improves store throughput by **18.8%**. |\\n| **Decoupled Pure Read** | Continuous readback pass + volatile sink update. | 114,000 ns (0.114 ms) | 1.14 ns / op | **4.33 cycles / op** | L1 data cache prefetcher streams 64-bit loads with sub-cycle instruction pipelining. |\\n| **SIMD 256-Bit Store** | Vectorized SIMD node pair stores (`u128` + `u128`). | 237,000 ns (0.237 ms) | 2.37 ns / 256B node | **9.01 cycles / node** | High-density 256-bit pay\n<truncated 1906 bytes>","Description":"\"Creating M4 Hardware Microarchitecture & STLF Audit Document\"","Overwrite":"true","TargetFile":"\"/Volumes/Untitled/SSOT_Agentic_Brain_v1.1.0_ENGINEERING_RELEASE/docs/audits/M4_HARDWARE_STLF_AND_MICROARCHITECTURE_AUDIT.md\"","toolAction":"\"Writing M4 hardware STLF audit document\"","toolSummary":"\"Writing M4 hardware STLF audit document\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
